1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,508
It's an art. It's a science. It's a marriage of vapor and spirits.

2
00:00:07,509 --> 00:00:10,920
From the elite to the illegal.

3
00:00:10,921 --> 00:00:13,740
From the ban to the celebrated.

4
00:00:13,741 --> 00:00:19,380
The distillation of spirits is a $100 billion a year business.

5
00:00:19,381 --> 00:00:28,200
Brandy, moonshine, absinthe, liqueurs, and even restaurant distilleries. Cheers!

6
00:00:28,201 --> 00:00:32,200
Now, Distilleries 2 on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,360
How do you capture magic in a bottle?

8
00:00:46,060 --> 00:00:48,940
For centuries, people have been obsessed with doing it.

9
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,960
The desire to distill and develop the perfect liquor knows no bounds.

10
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:57,600
Even time can be arrested and stored in a glass.

11
00:00:57,601 --> 00:00:59,140
if you have the know-how.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,440
Well, the cellar in the basement of our chateau

13
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:04,040
is the Paradise Cellar,

14
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:06,080
and this is a very important one for us

15
00:01:06,081 --> 00:01:08,800
where we have actually created a reserve

16
00:01:08,801 --> 00:01:11,040
of the very, very old cognacs.

17
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:14,240
These are cognacs that can go back over 200 years.

18
00:01:14,740 --> 00:01:17,540
The oldest date back to the end of the 18th century

19
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:18,780
to the French Revolution.

20
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:21,440
Since ancient times,

21
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,860
the process of distilling the spirits of natural ingredients

22
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:30,160
like fruits and grains, into alcohol has been both a science and a mystery.

23
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:36,800
Distilled spirits are called spirits because they represent the essence of what they're made from,

24
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:42,220
and so the whole idea of spirits was a little bit mystical.

25
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:45,400
The process itself is quite simple.

26
00:01:48,320 --> 00:01:51,820
In distillation, liquids are separated from each other

27
00:01:51,821 --> 00:01:54,160
when they're converted into vapor at different temperatures.

28
00:01:54,161 --> 00:02:04,794
In the case of fermented liquids, the distiller must keep his fermented liquids below water's boiling point of 212 degrees Fahrenheit in order for alcohol to boil off

29
00:02:04,795 --> 00:02:06,460
at 173 degrees Fahrenheit.

30
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:13,780
The alcohol vapor rises up into a condenser, where it cools and returns to a liquid state.

31
00:02:14,660 --> 00:02:17,660
This distilled alcohol is the basis for all liquor.

32
00:02:17,661 --> 00:02:24,980
From there, it will be bottled, aged, or mixed with other ingredients to create a specific alcoholic beverage.

33
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:29,700
One perennially popular spirit is brandy.

34
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:35,960
The word itself is derived from the Dutch word brandyveen, which means burnt wine.

35
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:44,860
Brandy is the distilled essence of an alcoholic fermented product made from, in most cases, fruit,

36
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:48,600
but also it can be made from other things as well.

37
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:51,600
The origins of brandy can be traced back

38
00:02:51,601 --> 00:02:53,320
to the Muslim Mediterranean states

39
00:02:53,321 --> 00:02:54,700
of the 7th and 8th centuries.

40
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,260
There, Arabian chemists experimented

41
00:02:59,261 --> 00:03:01,100
with distilling grapes and other fruits

42
00:03:01,101 --> 00:03:03,240
in order to make medicinal spirits.

43
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:07,580
However, the refined brandy that we know today

44
00:03:07,581 --> 00:03:10,140
was developed in the 16th century

45
00:03:10,141 --> 00:03:12,320
by Dutch and French wine merchants.

46
00:03:12,321 --> 00:03:18,560
It was started because the wine that was being made in the area which is now cognac,

47
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:26,060
because cognac was on the seacoast, it was being shipped to Holland where the Dutch drank the wine.

48
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:33,740
But they were finding that it was because of the long sea trip that the wine would not be so wonderful when it arrived in Holland

49
00:03:33,741 --> 00:03:37,360
and also it was very expensive to ship large quantities of it.

50
00:03:37,361 --> 00:03:41,720
so the wine producers got the brilliant idea of

51
00:03:41,721 --> 00:03:44,020
well why don't we just concentrate this wine

52
00:03:44,021 --> 00:03:45,860
why don't we distill it

53
00:03:45,861 --> 00:03:47,780
and then when it gets to the other side

54
00:03:47,781 --> 00:03:48,720
when it gets to Holland

55
00:03:48,721 --> 00:03:51,080
they'll just add water and they'll have the wine

56
00:03:51,081 --> 00:03:54,460
obviously you can't reconstitute wine by

57
00:03:54,461 --> 00:03:57,940
that's been distilled into wine again

58
00:03:57,941 --> 00:04:01,080
but when the distilled product got to Holland

59
00:04:01,081 --> 00:04:05,400
everybody said hey we really like this a lot better than the wine you were sending us

60
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:07,800
So keep sending this distilled stuff.

61
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:13,860
Today, brandy made of grapes remains the predominant favorite.

62
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:18,020
At the Christian Brothers Distillery in Central California,

63
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:20,960
the grapes are first fermented into wine,

64
00:04:21,420 --> 00:04:23,180
which is then distilled into brandy.

65
00:04:24,220 --> 00:04:27,160
This is the first step in the brandy process.

66
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:32,120
This is where we're bringing the wine into the distillery through our charge tanks.

67
00:04:32,121 --> 00:04:39,100
Our charge tanks are set up to splash rack the wine in and to aerate the wine at the same time.

68
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:44,240
What we're trying to do is remove any of the gases and trapped gases from fermentation.

69
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:49,220
This allows our still to balance out and run smooth and make a better brandy.

70
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:53,180
The distillers regulate every aspect of the process.

71
00:04:54,580 --> 00:04:58,620
What you're looking at here is a master control panel for our distillery.

72
00:04:58,621 --> 00:05:01,620
On this panel, the operators control all of the fluid

73
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:03,620
movement, all of the temperatures,

74
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:06,620
and all of the feed rates the product draws on the still

75
00:05:06,720 --> 00:05:10,360
in the brandy production process.

76
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:13,360
Christian Brothers uses a continuous rectifying still

77
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:15,840
to increase the flavor and complexity of the brandy

78
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:16,740
distillates.

79
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:24,380
First, the wine travels through a so-called stripper column.

80
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:28,620
Here, the alcohol boils to a vapor.

81
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:31,560
Then the vapor enters the rectifying column.

82
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:34,060
Inside, over two dozen copper plates

83
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:36,360
act like stacked stills to create

84
00:05:36,460 --> 00:05:38,960
their own separate distillation.

85
00:05:39,060 --> 00:05:43,360
Each section differs in temperature by about 1.5 degree.

86
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:48,380
The higher the plate, the higher the alcohol content.

87
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:51,480
About 3 quarters of the way up, the vapor is extracted

88
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:56,520
at just less than 170 proof, or 85% alcohol, which

89
00:05:56,521 --> 00:05:58,700
which is a legal requirement for producing brandy.

90
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:04,340
The remaining vapor will continue to rise until it condenses,

91
00:06:05,040 --> 00:06:08,700
following downpipes toward the bottom to begin the process again.

92
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:17,060
Brandy concentrates a good many of the congeners that are found in wine,

93
00:06:17,061 --> 00:06:20,840
the desirable attributes, and not just the alcohol, I must say,

94
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:24,900
but you're looking at the floral and the fruity essences that are in wine

95
00:06:24,901 --> 00:06:26,820
that are concentrated down.

96
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:31,980
The result is a sturdy, quality brandy at an affordable price.

97
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:38,400
But brandy also has a very desirable and very expensive elite relative

98
00:06:38,401 --> 00:06:40,160
called cognac.

99
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,560
It comes from the cognac region of France.

100
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:47,600
One of the premier distillers of the drink

101
00:06:47,601 --> 00:06:51,600
is the Courvoisier Company, established in 1843.

102
00:06:51,601 --> 00:06:56,500
The region itself makes a very specific type of brandy.

103
00:06:56,700 --> 00:07:00,980
Not all brandies are cognac, but of course cognac is always a brandy.

104
00:07:01,380 --> 00:07:05,000
And the reason for that is because we have a very specific type of soil,

105
00:07:05,140 --> 00:07:08,760
we have a very specific type of climate that create from the grapes

106
00:07:08,761 --> 00:07:13,620
exactly the right distillation to go into a very specific and special brandy.

107
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,820
Every aspect of the liquor and its distillation is controlled by a strict set of regulations,

108
00:07:19,821 --> 00:07:22,820
including the types of grapes that can be used.

109
00:07:22,821 --> 00:07:24,820
The main one is the Uniblanc,

110
00:07:24,821 --> 00:07:26,820
which of course they're all white grapes.

111
00:07:26,821 --> 00:07:29,820
It represents the very large, large majority,

112
00:07:29,821 --> 00:07:33,820
so probably 95%, 99% come from that area.

113
00:07:33,821 --> 00:07:35,820
But we also have the Forte Blanche and the Colombard,

114
00:07:35,821 --> 00:07:37,820
which can be used.

115
00:07:39,820 --> 00:07:42,820
Pierre Cernovich oversees quality control

116
00:07:42,821 --> 00:07:44,820
for Courvoisier Cognacs.

117
00:07:44,821 --> 00:07:46,820
A strict attention to detail is required

118
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:48,820
for every step of the process.

119
00:07:48,821 --> 00:07:50,820
including harvesting the grapes.

120
00:07:50,821 --> 00:07:54,820
We work with over 1,000 growers,

121
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:57,820
and every year, according to the specificity of the year,

122
00:07:57,821 --> 00:08:02,820
the weather, and we send advice to every growers to tell them,

123
00:08:02,821 --> 00:08:07,820
okay, this year you should start the harvest, not after this date.

124
00:08:09,820 --> 00:08:12,820
Once the grapes are harvested and fermented into wine,

125
00:08:12,821 --> 00:08:14,820
they're distilled in a pot still.

126
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:21,540
in a pot still. Pot stills are among the simplest of stills, yet meticulous

127
00:08:21,541 --> 00:08:25,800
cognac distillers prefer them over continuous stills. They're not very large,

128
00:08:25,801 --> 00:08:29,880
in fact we prefer to use smaller ones at Covoisier because they give you more

129
00:08:29,881 --> 00:08:34,620
control over the distilling process. This added control allows the distiller to

130
00:08:34,621 --> 00:08:38,460
isolate only the best part of the alcoholic vapor that is being turned into

131
00:08:38,461 --> 00:08:44,400
cognac. It's a tedious job, the distiller must closely monitor the still at all

132
00:08:44,401 --> 00:08:50,880
times cognac is one of the few brandies where the wine is distilled twice in

133
00:08:50,881 --> 00:08:57,840
order to wring every bit of flavor out of it the entire process takes 24 hours

134
00:08:57,841 --> 00:09:02,960
during every step of the process the young cognac is tested for quality

135
00:09:02,961 --> 00:09:07,240
surprisingly the scent of the liquid can be much more revealing than the taste

136
00:09:07,241 --> 00:09:13,140
of this stage the nose is the most important tool for the tasters of course

137
00:09:13,141 --> 00:09:16,660
We have a sip sometime, but when you train your nose for tasting,

138
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,700
you can detect a lot of different flavors.

139
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:24,400
When the distillate is approved, it's ready for aging.

140
00:09:24,920 --> 00:09:27,640
This is always done in custom-made oak barrels.

141
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:33,040
It's very expensive because this is 200-year-old oak that we're working with.

142
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:34,280
It's amazing.

143
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:36,200
When they start putting together a barrel,

144
00:09:36,540 --> 00:09:40,780
there is a whole series of staves that they just seem to pick one up after the other,

145
00:09:40,781 --> 00:09:46,500
and they seem to know how to select exactly the right number of staves to make a perfect circle.

146
00:09:47,060 --> 00:09:51,700
There are never any nails involved, and what they do is they shape this over a fire.

147
00:09:52,200 --> 00:09:54,680
So while they are putting together the circle,

148
00:09:55,060 --> 00:09:59,700
they are also starting to pull in the base so that they're actually rounding it correctly.

149
00:10:00,320 --> 00:10:04,100
And when they get to the end of the process, they always have a perfect barrel.

150
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:10,060
Such care is given to the barrels because it's here where the cognac's main qualities

151
00:10:10,061 --> 00:10:11,360
are brought out over time.

152
00:10:13,100 --> 00:10:14,620
Because wood is porous,

153
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:16,980
it allows air to combine with the spirit,

154
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,180
permitting a slow oxidation that creates flavor.

155
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:25,740
Contact with the wood itself also affects flavor,

156
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:29,280
as well as giving the cognac its distinct, rich color.

157
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:33,160
Cognac can be aged anywhere from three years

158
00:10:33,161 --> 00:10:36,520
up to 60, 70, 80 years.

159
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:39,800
Now, normally, when it gets up to over 50 years,

160
00:10:39,801 --> 00:10:41,720
anywhere from 50 to 70, 75.

161
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:45,300
Then we hit the peak when it really is at its best.

162
00:10:45,480 --> 00:10:47,600
And we're very careful at that time

163
00:10:47,601 --> 00:10:50,660
to take it out of the barrel and put it into glass

164
00:10:50,661 --> 00:10:52,600
where it's not going to change anymore.

165
00:10:53,580 --> 00:10:56,100
Cognac aged in oak for 50 years or more

166
00:10:56,101 --> 00:10:58,540
can cost over $5,000 a bottle.

167
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:02,760
Fortunately, not all of Courvoisier's cognacs are so expensive.

168
00:11:04,680 --> 00:11:07,400
The final step in cognac making is the blending.

169
00:11:07,401 --> 00:11:13,540
Each new batch of cognac varies in taste from year to year and vintage to vintage.

170
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:19,180
Yet those who buy Courvoisier Cognacs expect the flavor to be consistent.

171
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:25,020
It's up to a master blender to take batches from different years

172
00:11:25,021 --> 00:11:28,820
and blend them so they taste exactly like what the consumers expect.

173
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:35,300
He sometimes goes to the Courvoisier Chateau's cognac seller to review past blends.

174
00:11:35,301 --> 00:11:40,980
We don't actually have a recipe, and I think that's why the master blender and his blending

175
00:11:40,981 --> 00:11:43,096
team become even more important.

176
00:11:43,097 --> 00:11:47,460
He has to really every year determine what are the changes that he

177
00:11:47,461 --> 00:11:51,698
has to make to continue to have the same quality every year.

178
00:11:51,699 --> 00:11:54,100
The cognac in those bottles can be

179
00:11:54,101 --> 00:11:59,240
very important for us in future years to understanding what cognac was, where it came from.

180
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:04,540
Once the master blender makes his decisions, various batches from different years are blended

181
00:12:04,541 --> 00:12:08,640
together in huge vats to make exactly the right tasting cognac.

182
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:14,120
It's our goal to produce the best cognac in the world.

183
00:12:14,360 --> 00:12:20,980
We are one of the best because each product has a special character, has a special style.

184
00:12:21,780 --> 00:12:25,060
Cognac is considered to be one of the world's most refined liquors.

185
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:31,760
From the other side of the Atlantic in the deep south comes another classic, anything

186
00:12:31,761 --> 00:12:32,780
but refined.

187
00:12:32,781 --> 00:12:36,200
Instead, it's rowdy, rip-roaring and illegal.

188
00:12:37,040 --> 00:12:39,380
White Lightning, Moonshine.

189
00:12:40,260 --> 00:12:46,900
95% of cognac is exported from France and marketed to over 155 countries.

190
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:52,020
We now return to distilleries on Modern Marvels.

191
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:55,420
They call it White Lightning.

192
00:12:56,640 --> 00:12:57,840
Moonshine whiskey.

193
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:00,800
Strong enough to quench the devil's thirst.

194
00:13:00,801 --> 00:13:03,800
its home is the backwoods of rural America.

195
00:13:05,800 --> 00:13:07,800
This illegal liquor, made of corn,

196
00:13:07,801 --> 00:13:11,800
has been a source of legend, controversy, and rebellion

197
00:13:11,801 --> 00:13:14,800
since before the founding of the country.

198
00:13:14,801 --> 00:13:18,800
Well, it was fun to make this whiskey by the light of the moon,

199
00:13:18,801 --> 00:13:21,800
and it was, of course, called Moonshine because it was made at night

200
00:13:21,801 --> 00:13:23,800
and it was in secret locations.

201
00:13:23,801 --> 00:13:25,800
They had to be well away from buildings,

202
00:13:25,801 --> 00:13:28,800
away from roads and houses where they might be found out.

203
00:13:28,801 --> 00:13:38,992
The whole concept of distilled spirits in the United States was brought here by Scots-Irish people who came to America to avoid excessive dealings with the government

204
00:13:38,993 --> 00:13:41,080
in the places that they came from.

205
00:13:41,500 --> 00:13:46,800
And they started making whiskey, which is what they had made where they came from.

206
00:13:47,460 --> 00:13:52,660
And of course the government is always interested in getting revenue from distillation.

207
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:55,780
The whiskey makers didn't see it that way.

208
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:01,540
In 1794, they met the government with armed resistance in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania,

209
00:14:02,040 --> 00:14:04,440
in what became known as the Whiskey Rebellion.

210
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:11,620
President George Washington had to mobilize a militia army of nearly 13,000 troops to put it down.

211
00:14:13,560 --> 00:14:18,600
The Moonshiners' resistance soon collapsed, but not their desire to make white lightning.

212
00:14:20,980 --> 00:14:25,580
Attempting to avoid the tax collector, many of them sought refuge in wilderness areas,

213
00:14:25,780 --> 00:14:28,180
especially in remote areas of the Deep South.

214
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:34,860
During the Civil War, they supplied many a soldier

215
00:14:34,861 --> 00:14:37,020
on both the southern and northern sides

216
00:14:37,021 --> 00:14:40,600
with courage in a bottle before major battles.

217
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:46,100
When the United States banned the legal sale of liquor in 1920,

218
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:50,680
creating the Prohibition era, the moonshine business boomed.

219
00:14:51,740 --> 00:14:53,940
White lightning became a favorite drink

220
00:14:53,941 --> 00:14:55,660
in speakeasies all over America.

221
00:14:55,780 --> 00:15:02,960
The boom didn't last, though, and when Prohibition was repealed in 1933,

222
00:15:03,800 --> 00:15:08,920
the Moonshiners had to face the same stark financial realities as the rest of the country,

223
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:12,000
which had already plunged into the Great Depression.

224
00:15:13,900 --> 00:15:16,820
No longer able to make big money from speakeasies,

225
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:21,720
the Shiners were still able to make survival money in areas where most jobs had dried up.

226
00:15:21,721 --> 00:15:25,400
Money was tight.

227
00:15:25,401 --> 00:15:29,760
You just, you couldn't make any money at anything.

228
00:15:29,761 --> 00:15:31,560
It was hard times.

229
00:15:31,561 --> 00:15:33,960
You could make a little moonshine.

230
00:15:33,961 --> 00:15:39,840
In some families, the tradition of distilling white lightning goes back generations.

231
00:15:39,841 --> 00:15:44,480
I guess I was probably six, seven-year-old.

232
00:15:44,481 --> 00:15:47,480
The first steel I ever went to was with my daddy.

233
00:15:47,481 --> 00:15:50,860
I learned it from my daddy, and he learned it from his daddy,

234
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,360
and so on down the line.

235
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:56,100
Besides the extra money the liquor brought in,

236
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:58,700
many moonshiners found the job exciting.

237
00:15:59,260 --> 00:16:04,860
I was just a kid then, and just the thrill of it,

238
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:07,680
the thrill of getting away.

239
00:16:08,280 --> 00:16:09,120
Yeah, I liked it.

240
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:11,360
I'd rather do that than anything I ever done.

241
00:16:11,361 --> 00:16:17,980
Although it's still illegal to sell or make white lightning,

242
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:20,160
you can find moonshiners today,

243
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:22,660
distilling just like their ancestors did.

244
00:16:26,380 --> 00:16:29,380
Dwight Bearden comes from a strong moonshine tradition.

245
00:16:30,220 --> 00:16:32,620
He couldn't take us to an actual working still,

246
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:34,840
but agreed to demonstrate the process,

247
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:37,820
the same way he does at county fairs and folk festivals.

248
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:47,960
And this distillery here is roughly 80-year-old that belonged to my great-uncle.

249
00:16:49,080 --> 00:16:52,900
At the heart of the moonshine process is making the mash.

250
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:59,440
This is the two main ingredients for making corn mash, cornmeal and corn malt.

251
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:03,420
You mix these together with water, and this is what they make.

252
00:17:04,300 --> 00:17:08,460
This is corn mash after it's been fermented for seven days.

253
00:17:08,461 --> 00:17:10,240
This is ready for the steel.

254
00:17:11,360 --> 00:17:18,980
Today, non-traditionalist moonshiners are also using low-protein commercial hog chow as a replacement for cornmeal,

255
00:17:19,220 --> 00:17:22,180
because it's so readily available and doesn't have to be milled.

256
00:17:23,940 --> 00:17:28,320
Moonshine distillation is simple. The art is in the execution.

257
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:30,520
You pour the mash in.

258
00:17:33,700 --> 00:17:39,120
Then you stir until it comes to a boil.

259
00:17:39,121 --> 00:17:44,880
You put the cap on.

260
00:17:44,881 --> 00:17:48,200
Cap arm fits into your thump barrel.

261
00:17:48,201 --> 00:17:52,740
Caps goes on.

262
00:17:52,741 --> 00:17:57,540
And you seal the cracks with inner tube.

263
00:17:57,541 --> 00:17:59,660
You boil the mash in the steel.

264
00:17:59,661 --> 00:18:05,780
The mash comes up, the steam comes up through your cap, through your cap arm, into your

265
00:18:05,781 --> 00:18:06,780
thump barrel.

266
00:18:06,781 --> 00:18:10,000
The thump barrel is named for the thumping sound it makes.

267
00:18:11,320 --> 00:18:15,040
It's designed to catch any impurities that may have come through from the mash.

268
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:23,500
If you run the steel a little bit too hard, get the fire just a little bit too hot and it boils too much, too fast,

269
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:26,820
it will boil over in what they call puke.

270
00:18:27,100 --> 00:18:31,080
And it would puke over into your condenser if you didn't have the thump barrel.

271
00:18:31,340 --> 00:18:32,780
The thump barrel catches it.

272
00:18:32,781 --> 00:18:37,780
Next, the alcohol steam continues on to the copper worm.

273
00:18:37,781 --> 00:18:41,780
The steam comes up through your condenser pipe.

274
00:18:41,781 --> 00:18:46,780
The condenser is in cold, in water, and you've got cold water running in,

275
00:18:46,781 --> 00:18:49,780
and the steam is making the water hot.

276
00:18:49,781 --> 00:18:52,780
You've got hot water coming out over here.

277
00:18:52,781 --> 00:18:56,780
And then right here, it turns the steam back into liquid,

278
00:18:56,781 --> 00:18:59,780
and this is your alcohol. This is your moonshine right here.

279
00:18:59,781 --> 00:19:07,780
here moonshine is the ultimate no frills alcohol it's rarely aged and is ready to drink right out

280
00:19:07,781 --> 00:19:15,140
of the still because it won't spend any time in a cask it won't take on any color so it remains clear

281
00:19:16,980 --> 00:19:23,780
that's why they call it white lightning from the end of prohibition to the 1950s in the large cities

282
00:19:23,781 --> 00:19:27,988
of the South, some people still had a taste for moonshine whiskey.

283
00:19:27,989 --> 00:19:29,200
Drivers were needed

284
00:19:29,201 --> 00:19:31,960
to transport the shine in souped-up cars.

285
00:19:31,961 --> 00:19:37,900
If you was a whiskey hauler, you was kind of a little bit of hero up in this part of

286
00:19:37,901 --> 00:19:43,800
the country, and most of the young boys, that was a way of life with them.

287
00:19:43,801 --> 00:19:48,441
It may have been Southern Hill country glamorous, but it was dangerous work.

288
00:19:48,442 --> 00:19:49,540
Around any corner,

289
00:19:49,541 --> 00:19:51,620
federal revenuers might be waiting.

290
00:19:51,621 --> 00:19:56,120
The revenuers were like the G-men of the prohibition period.

291
00:19:56,121 --> 00:19:57,400
They were tough.

292
00:19:57,401 --> 00:19:58,620
They carried guns.

293
00:19:58,621 --> 00:20:00,760
They went out to get money, and they didn't want

294
00:20:00,761 --> 00:20:02,160
to take no for an answer.

295
00:20:02,161 --> 00:20:05,700
If you had a fast car, you could haul contingents

296
00:20:05,701 --> 00:20:07,660
and not lose any money.

297
00:20:07,661 --> 00:20:11,580
But if you had a slow car, they'd catch you and take your car

298
00:20:11,581 --> 00:20:13,740
and you whisk you and put you in jail.

299
00:20:13,741 --> 00:20:17,640
And of course, right away, we figured the best thing to do

300
00:20:17,641 --> 00:20:20,340
is always have the fastest car and be the best driver.

301
00:20:21,620 --> 00:20:25,940
To avoid detection, the moonshiners did everything they could to make their cars look normal.

302
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:31,840
You put big springs, big shocks, stiffened everything up,

303
00:20:31,841 --> 00:20:36,980
and when you had a load on it, it looked just like it was normal going down the road.

304
00:20:39,560 --> 00:20:44,080
A spectacular offshoot of the moonshine business was the development of stock car racing.

305
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:51,180
One young moonshine driver was Junior Johnson, who later became a NASCAR champion.

306
00:20:51,620 --> 00:20:56,300
Most of the guys originally started out in NASCAR,

307
00:20:56,400 --> 00:20:57,820
the bootlegging side of it,

308
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,820
was for bragging rights more than anything else.

309
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:02,340
There wasn't any money much in it.

310
00:21:03,400 --> 00:21:05,320
Everything you'd learn in a whiskey business

311
00:21:05,321 --> 00:21:07,060
would adapt right to the race track.

312
00:21:09,260 --> 00:21:11,280
The high octane of NASCAR

313
00:21:11,281 --> 00:21:14,040
and the high proof of white lightning

314
00:21:14,041 --> 00:21:18,280
had nothing on a 19th century drink called absinthe.

315
00:21:18,800 --> 00:21:21,600
In alcohol content and in the trouble it stirred up,

316
00:21:21,620 --> 00:21:26,300
The Parisian's counterpart to American white lightning was the Green Fairy.

317
00:21:28,240 --> 00:21:38,120
Besides white lightning, other slang for moonshine are hooch, mountain dew, stump hole water, liquid stump blaster, mule kick, and white mule.

318
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:39,560
This modern...

319
00:21:39,561 --> 00:21:43,260
We now return to distilleries on Modern Marvels.

320
00:21:45,640 --> 00:21:47,980
They call it the Green Fairy.

321
00:21:47,981 --> 00:21:56,420
Absinthe is a highly alcoholic, distilled, and is flavored spirit, reputed to have psychotropic

322
00:21:56,421 --> 00:21:59,200
qualities.

323
00:21:59,201 --> 00:22:03,580
In the history of liquors, it holds a special place for being one of the most controversial

324
00:22:03,581 --> 00:22:06,080
drinks of all time.

325
00:22:06,081 --> 00:22:08,340
Yet it wasn't always that way.

326
00:22:08,341 --> 00:22:12,920
For a period during the late 19th century, absinthe was one of the most popular liquors

327
00:22:12,921 --> 00:22:15,400
in the Western world.

328
00:22:15,401 --> 00:22:20,880
like Perneau-Fisse produced over 30,000 liters of it a day.

329
00:22:20,881 --> 00:22:26,400
American chemist T.A. Brough has spent years researching the drink.

330
00:22:26,401 --> 00:22:31,040
Absinthe was originally developed as a medicine.

331
00:22:31,041 --> 00:22:37,540
It was developed as a digestive aid in the late, late 1700s.

332
00:22:37,541 --> 00:22:43,540
And it really wasn't until the 1840s when the French government dispensed rations of

333
00:22:43,541 --> 00:22:47,500
of absinthe to its soldiers abroad, like in North Africa.

334
00:22:47,501 --> 00:22:52,360
It was given to them as a means of purifying, unclean water.

335
00:22:52,361 --> 00:22:53,740
And when they went back to France,

336
00:22:53,741 --> 00:22:57,260
they brought this taste for absinthe back with them.

337
00:22:57,261 --> 00:23:00,780
By the late 19th century, it became the in drink,

338
00:23:00,781 --> 00:23:04,120
especially amongst artists and intellectuals.

339
00:23:04,121 --> 00:23:07,320
Vincent van Gogh used it frequently.

340
00:23:07,321 --> 00:23:11,660
Pablo Picasso painted absinthe-themed works,

341
00:23:11,661 --> 00:23:12,940
as did Edgar Degas.

342
00:23:15,160 --> 00:23:18,800
Toulouse-Lautrec used it and even taught his pet cormorant to drink it.

343
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:24,400
Its downward slide began with a push from the wine industry,

344
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:27,420
which tried to make it look like the devil in a bottle.

345
00:23:27,940 --> 00:23:31,140
Many people aren't aware that back in the late 1800s,

346
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:35,520
the French wine industry was almost completely wiped out

347
00:23:35,521 --> 00:23:37,960
by an infestation of an insect.

348
00:23:37,961 --> 00:23:43,480
And it's during this time when absinthe experienced a tremendous rise in popularity.

349
00:23:44,080 --> 00:23:49,900
When the French wine industry began to make a comeback and get back on their feet,

350
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:50,700
what happened?

351
00:23:50,701 --> 00:23:54,620
They found themselves suddenly opposed to this green liquor,

352
00:23:54,980 --> 00:23:57,680
which was proclaimed to be the national drink of France.

353
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:00,480
And they found ways to demonize absinthe.

354
00:24:01,500 --> 00:24:04,940
Opponents of the drink claimed it rotted your brain, and worse.

355
00:24:05,780 --> 00:24:08,640
Yeah, just like reefer madness.

356
00:24:09,040 --> 00:24:13,420
For a period, it was decided that absinthe had some ingredients

357
00:24:13,421 --> 00:24:17,200
that were deleterious to your health,

358
00:24:17,880 --> 00:24:20,420
that it was in fact poisonous.

359
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:26,640
Modern research has shown that the probable cause of absinthe's horrible reputation

360
00:24:26,641 --> 00:24:30,780
was due to the fact that many people were drinking inferior brands of the liquor,

361
00:24:30,781 --> 00:24:35,220
which contained harmful substances such as copper sulfate.

362
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:40,720
This chemical created a vivid green color in the liquor

363
00:24:40,721 --> 00:24:42,500
but was also poisonous.

364
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:47,580
High-quality absinthe didn't seem to have the same dangers.

365
00:24:48,820 --> 00:24:50,980
Nevertheless, the damage was done.

366
00:24:52,280 --> 00:24:56,860
Absinthe was banned in Switzerland in 1910,

367
00:24:57,380 --> 00:24:59,160
the U.S. in 1912,

368
00:24:59,161 --> 00:25:03,020
and eventually and reluctantly in France

369
00:25:03,021 --> 00:25:05,520
just prior to the onset of the First World War.

370
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:10,160
Today, it's regaining legal legitimacy and popularity

371
00:25:10,161 --> 00:25:13,360
in such countries as Germany, Spain, and France.

372
00:25:16,240 --> 00:25:17,860
Pernod and the Combier distilleries

373
00:25:17,861 --> 00:25:19,040
are getting back in the business,

374
00:25:19,540 --> 00:25:20,940
along with many new players.

375
00:25:22,300 --> 00:25:25,500
Perhaps the most significant ban in absinthe,

376
00:25:25,640 --> 00:25:29,020
which was in most of Western Europe, is gone.

377
00:25:29,160 --> 00:25:35,000
since the European Union has standardized all its food and beverage laws

378
00:25:35,001 --> 00:25:36,640
and re-legalized absinthe.

379
00:25:36,641 --> 00:25:40,720
But the irony here is that traditional absinthe, we know,

380
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:44,840
actually meets modern quality standards.

381
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:46,660
But not in the United States.

382
00:25:47,420 --> 00:25:50,400
The current status of absinthe in the United States,

383
00:25:50,580 --> 00:25:52,200
absinthe remains in sort of a limbo

384
00:25:52,201 --> 00:25:55,620
because the regulations that would define the content of absinthe

385
00:25:55,621 --> 00:25:57,620
are somewhat ambiguous and vague.

386
00:25:57,621 --> 00:26:01,680
and effectively the U.S. government has taken a stance

387
00:26:01,681 --> 00:26:04,580
that absinthe is more or less a beverage

388
00:26:04,581 --> 00:26:07,180
that would not be approved by the FDA

389
00:26:07,181 --> 00:26:09,560
for distribution for sale in the United States.

390
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:10,980
That may change at some point,

391
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:13,760
but that's the status quo as of today.

392
00:26:15,760 --> 00:26:19,820
In the town of Saumur, located in France's Loire Valley,

393
00:26:20,780 --> 00:26:23,240
Brough is distilling an upscale version of the drink

394
00:26:23,241 --> 00:26:24,540
at the Combier Distillery.

395
00:26:24,541 --> 00:26:32,880
I was very curious to determine through modern chemistry, forensic chemistry essentially,

396
00:26:33,220 --> 00:26:41,360
and find out exactly what was in this liquor and was there anything in it that I could find using modern chemistry

397
00:26:41,361 --> 00:26:46,680
that would prove that the allegations of its deleterious effects were true.

398
00:26:46,740 --> 00:26:48,060
And I was unable to find anything.

399
00:26:48,061 --> 00:26:55,200
Bro's initial research concerning the drink was made doubly hard because high-quality absinthe

400
00:26:55,201 --> 00:26:58,560
has been scarce since the drink was banned nearly a century ago.

401
00:27:00,680 --> 00:27:03,620
Accurate recipes for the liquor are hard to come by.

402
00:27:04,620 --> 00:27:12,300
My initial attempts at crafting absinthe on a small scale were based upon century-old

403
00:27:12,301 --> 00:27:15,620
recipes that were a paragraph or two of information.

404
00:27:15,621 --> 00:27:21,720
and while initially I had no way to determine how successful they were

405
00:27:21,721 --> 00:27:30,320
until I was able to get in my hands several bottles of the finest absents from a century ago that were unopened.

406
00:27:30,860 --> 00:27:35,960
And when I analyzed and tasted those, I realized that, wow, I was way off base.

407
00:27:35,961 --> 00:27:40,780
It took me quite some time to be able to reverse engineer those original absents

408
00:27:40,781 --> 00:27:43,800
and rediscover the lost art of absentecrafting.

409
00:27:44,800 --> 00:27:46,540
Once Bro perfected the formula,

410
00:27:46,760 --> 00:27:49,360
he became obsessed with every aspect of the process.

411
00:27:50,420 --> 00:27:54,300
Everything that I do in crafting my absinthe

412
00:27:54,301 --> 00:27:58,240
is exactly the way that it was done in the 19th century.

413
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:02,660
Even the Combier distillery is connected to the 19th century.

414
00:28:03,340 --> 00:28:06,560
Part of it was designed by the legendary French engineer,

415
00:28:06,980 --> 00:28:07,780
Gustave Eiffel.

416
00:28:08,580 --> 00:28:12,380
We are very linked with the history,

417
00:28:12,381 --> 00:28:15,380
and we want to keep like it was before.

418
00:28:16,260 --> 00:28:19,140
We know Eiffel for the Eiffel Tower, of course,

419
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:21,660
but he made many, many of the things.

420
00:28:22,360 --> 00:28:24,220
He designed the main part of the distillery.

421
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:26,840
It's very important for us today

422
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:29,020
because when you go in the distillery,

423
00:28:29,380 --> 00:28:32,360
you see this beautiful Eiffel architecture.

424
00:28:35,140 --> 00:28:37,100
The ingredients for his absinthe

425
00:28:37,101 --> 00:28:39,280
are grown to Brough's exact specifications.

426
00:28:39,281 --> 00:28:43,280
They are herbs that don't contribute to the alcoholic content,

427
00:28:43,281 --> 00:28:49,280
but flavor the drink, and some say add that green fairy to the mix.

428
00:28:49,281 --> 00:28:54,280
Here we have the holy trinity of herbs that are used in making absinthe.

429
00:28:54,281 --> 00:28:59,280
First we have artemisia absintheum, which is commonly known as grand wormwood.

430
00:28:59,281 --> 00:29:02,280
This is the single most important herb in making absinthe.

431
00:29:02,281 --> 00:29:06,280
This herb is grown for me out near the Swiss border.

432
00:29:06,281 --> 00:29:14,260
Swiss border. Next herb we have is green anise from Spain. This herb nowadays is

433
00:29:14,261 --> 00:29:18,160
mostly used by the pharmaceutical industry, but this herb gives absinthe

434
00:29:18,161 --> 00:29:24,580
its characteristic anise flavor and aroma. And finally we have fennel. This is

435
00:29:24,581 --> 00:29:29,600
a sweet fennel from Provence. The first part of the distillation process

436
00:29:29,601 --> 00:29:34,160
involves maceration of the ingredients. This is the beginning of the maceration

437
00:29:34,161 --> 00:29:38,160
Here we add herbs like this wormwood here to distill.

438
00:29:38,260 --> 00:29:40,960
After we add our herbs to distill, we add alcohol.

439
00:29:41,060 --> 00:29:44,400
Alcohol extracts the essences from the herbs,

440
00:29:44,500 --> 00:29:47,100
and this is allowed to take place over a certain period of time

441
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:50,920
depending upon the type of absinthe that would make.

442
00:29:51,020 --> 00:29:53,420
Once the essences from the herbs are extracted,

443
00:29:53,520 --> 00:29:57,920
the still is heated and the alcohol is condensed.

444
00:29:58,020 --> 00:30:00,120
Before we distill the absinthe,

445
00:30:00,220 --> 00:30:04,160
if one were to taste the maceration of herbs and herbs,

446
00:30:04,161 --> 00:30:09,498
alcohol that's present before we distill, it's absolutely undrinkable.

447
00:30:09,499 --> 00:30:11,100
It's horribly bitter.

448
00:30:11,320 --> 00:30:12,626
It's a nasty mess.

449
00:30:12,627 --> 00:30:17,560
However, once the distillation starts and the distillate comes over,

450
00:30:18,120 --> 00:30:25,061
the result from the distillation process is this sweet, beautiful, herbal perfume.

451
00:30:25,062 --> 00:30:25,762
It's

452
00:30:25,763 --> 00:30:30,797
like bottled alpine springtime. The distillate is condensed and collected.

453
00:30:30,798 --> 00:30:32,620
Next, it will be colored.

454
00:30:34,160 --> 00:30:39,160
One method of coloring absinthe involves taking a special blend of proprietary coloring herbs,

455
00:30:39,161 --> 00:30:45,160
putting them into a giant tea bag, and soaking them into the instilled absinthe.

456
00:30:45,161 --> 00:30:51,160
After coloring, the drink is blended, bottled, and ready for consumption.

457
00:30:51,161 --> 00:30:55,160
And now I'm going to demonstrate the proper way to prepare an absinthe drink,

458
00:30:55,161 --> 00:30:57,160
as done in the traditional manner.

459
00:30:57,161 --> 00:30:59,160
The first thing we do is take our absinthe glass.

460
00:30:59,161 --> 00:31:02,160
In this case, we have a two-piece glass called a brewery glass.

461
00:31:02,161 --> 00:31:07,160
And to this glass, we pour about an ounce and a half of our absinthe,

462
00:31:07,161 --> 00:31:12,160
and we take the top portion of the glass and fill it with ice.

463
00:31:12,161 --> 00:31:16,160
And to the top portion of the glass, we add cool water,

464
00:31:16,161 --> 00:31:20,160
which drips through the ice slowly, gently,

465
00:31:20,161 --> 00:31:25,160
creating the clouding effect that is associated with the quality absinthe.

466
00:31:25,161 --> 00:31:30,160
And what's happening is the cool water is driving out the essences

467
00:31:30,161 --> 00:31:34,400
the essences that are dissolved in the pure liquor.

468
00:31:34,401 --> 00:31:36,160
And once enough water is added,

469
00:31:36,161 --> 00:31:39,040
just so that the entire drink becomes cloudy,

470
00:31:39,041 --> 00:31:42,780
then our absinthe is finished and is ready to drink.

471
00:31:42,781 --> 00:31:45,280
Santé.

472
00:31:45,281 --> 00:31:47,720
On the sweeter end of the distillery business

473
00:31:47,721 --> 00:31:50,580
are the liqueurs.

474
00:31:50,581 --> 00:31:54,120
More prim, proper, and legal than absinthe,

475
00:31:54,121 --> 00:31:57,360
they're created to start or end a fine meal.

476
00:31:57,361 --> 00:32:05,600
In 1878, 8 million liters of absinthe were imported into the United States.

477
00:32:06,880 --> 00:32:10,440
We now return to distilleries on Modern Marvels.

478
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:16,560
Of all spirits, liqueurs are the most diverse and are among the oldest.

479
00:32:17,280 --> 00:32:22,420
They also, more than any other drink, epitomize the idea of what spirits really are.

480
00:32:22,421 --> 00:32:25,420
That was the idea of liqueurs to begin with,

481
00:32:25,520 --> 00:32:28,960
that they concentrated the essence of some flavor

482
00:32:29,060 --> 00:32:30,900
or combination of flavors.

483
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:34,400
A peach liqueur, for example, in the wintertime,

484
00:32:34,500 --> 00:32:38,400
is a wonderful way to remember how good a fresh peach tastes

485
00:32:38,500 --> 00:32:40,540
in the summertime.

486
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:42,600
With their lively mixture of alcohol

487
00:32:42,700 --> 00:32:45,280
and natural ingredients, the sky's the limit

488
00:32:45,380 --> 00:32:47,340
when it comes to the flavors of liqueurs.

489
00:32:47,440 --> 00:32:49,440
And the way that you can categorize them

490
00:32:49,441 --> 00:32:56,440
categorize them as nut beans and herbs. That would be like hazelnuts or coffee.

491
00:32:56,441 --> 00:33:01,120
Fruit liqueurs, that's probably the largest group. There are liqueurs made

492
00:33:01,121 --> 00:33:04,680
from virtually every fruit you can think of.

493
00:33:04,681 --> 00:33:11,340
Liqueurs really stem from drinks that have been made since before 800 BC that

494
00:33:11,341 --> 00:33:18,360
involved herbs and fruits and so on. They became distilled starting in the

495
00:33:18,361 --> 00:33:24,660
Middle Ages and that's the time that some of these monks for example would

496
00:33:24,661 --> 00:33:30,960
produce herbal liqueurs that were essentially developed as medicinal

497
00:33:30,961 --> 00:33:37,880
remedies. During the 1500s style arbitrators began to champion them. Catherine

498
00:33:37,881 --> 00:33:44,240
de' Medici was a great lover of liqueurs. She was instrumental in making

499
00:33:44,241 --> 00:33:46,240
making them popular with many, many people.

500
00:33:46,241 --> 00:33:50,240
Liqueurs traveled from the West to the East

501
00:33:50,241 --> 00:33:52,240
when Peter the Great of Russia introduced them

502
00:33:52,241 --> 00:33:54,240
to his royal court in 1696.

503
00:33:57,240 --> 00:34:01,240
By the 20th century, their popularity grew worldwide,

504
00:34:01,241 --> 00:34:04,240
especially amongst women who preferred their sweetness

505
00:34:04,241 --> 00:34:06,240
to other spirits.

506
00:34:06,241 --> 00:34:10,240
Today, they're popular with both men and women,

507
00:34:10,241 --> 00:34:13,240
especially since they've revolutionized the world of cocktails.

508
00:34:14,240 --> 00:34:18,780
And there's no doubt that today's cocktails exist because of the liqueurs.

509
00:34:18,781 --> 00:34:20,940
I mean, that really is the key flavor component.

510
00:34:21,120 --> 00:34:23,600
Otherwise, you're just drinking straight vodka or rum or tequila.

511
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:26,880
There's nothing wrong with that, but if you really wanted something that tasted beyond that,

512
00:34:26,940 --> 00:34:29,380
you have to go to the liqueurs to add that flavor to the drinks.

513
00:34:32,520 --> 00:34:37,220
Giant liqueur producers like Hiram Walker make many different flavored liqueurs,

514
00:34:37,860 --> 00:34:39,180
blended to please any palate.

515
00:34:39,181 --> 00:34:44,920
The ingredients are mixed together at their Fort Smith, Arkansas facility,

516
00:34:45,060 --> 00:34:48,700
but they arrive by the tank load from all over the world.

517
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:54,400
We receive ingredients from across North America and as far away as France for some of our products.

518
00:34:55,780 --> 00:34:59,720
One of the key elements for making liqueurs is neutral grain spirits,

519
00:34:59,980 --> 00:35:02,580
which is the base alcohol that goes into the drink.

520
00:35:04,360 --> 00:35:07,060
Hiram Walker distills it in Lawrenceburg, Indiana,

521
00:35:07,061 --> 00:35:09,500
and ships it to their Fort Smith plant.

522
00:35:10,220 --> 00:35:12,100
This is where the whole process starts.

523
00:35:12,380 --> 00:35:14,840
This room can hold up to 308,000 gallons.

524
00:35:15,160 --> 00:35:16,900
We hold it here until we require it

525
00:35:16,901 --> 00:35:19,100
for making up liqueur compounds,

526
00:35:19,200 --> 00:35:21,580
and we pump it from here over to our blending tanks.

527
00:35:23,420 --> 00:35:26,220
Sugar is also delivered to the plant by the tank load.

528
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:30,100
Sugar is a very key ingredient in the making of a liqueur.

529
00:35:30,101 --> 00:35:32,480
By definition, a liqueur has to have

530
00:35:32,481 --> 00:35:34,400
a minimum 2.5% sugar content.

531
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:36,260
This is a sugar mixing tank

532
00:35:36,261 --> 00:35:38,660
where we take the granular sugar, mix it with water,

533
00:35:38,780 --> 00:35:40,120
and turn it into a liquid sucrose.

534
00:35:41,620 --> 00:35:44,120
The ingredients that make each liqueur unique

535
00:35:44,121 --> 00:35:45,300
are the exotic flavors.

536
00:35:46,640 --> 00:35:49,520
The art form for liqueur really comes with the blending

537
00:35:49,521 --> 00:35:52,200
and the adding of the natural flavoring components.

538
00:35:52,860 --> 00:35:54,280
This is our flavor storage room

539
00:35:54,281 --> 00:35:57,220
where we store drums of very concentrated flavors

540
00:35:57,221 --> 00:35:58,800
that we get from our flavor houses

541
00:35:58,801 --> 00:36:00,620
that provide the ingredients that we need

542
00:36:00,621 --> 00:36:02,160
to make all of our liqueurs.

543
00:36:02,500 --> 00:36:04,500
Some of these drums can be worth up to $5,000,

544
00:36:04,501 --> 00:36:05,740
and when this room is full,

545
00:36:05,741 --> 00:36:08,100
It's worth about $2 million with the flavors in here.

546
00:36:09,140 --> 00:36:12,340
Here you'll find everything from vanilla from Madagascar

547
00:36:12,341 --> 00:36:15,740
to coffee from Brazil and chocolate from Africa.

548
00:36:16,440 --> 00:36:18,740
We really look not only in the spurts industry,

549
00:36:18,920 --> 00:36:21,420
but everywhere in the food category and the juice category

550
00:36:21,421 --> 00:36:22,740
to see what's becoming popular.

551
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:25,280
This is one of the flavors we use in our liqueurs.

552
00:36:25,600 --> 00:36:28,200
This one happens to be peppermint oil that we use in peppermint schnapps.

553
00:36:28,700 --> 00:36:30,060
When we are going to make up a batch,

554
00:36:30,140 --> 00:36:33,060
we calculate the size of the batch that we need to meet our production needs,

555
00:36:33,061 --> 00:36:35,740
and from that we can calculate how many of these canisters

556
00:36:35,741 --> 00:36:36,980
we'd have to dump into the tank.

557
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:41,240
Each of these tanks contain an agitator

558
00:36:41,241 --> 00:36:43,720
to properly blend flavors into the mix.

559
00:36:46,440 --> 00:36:48,440
Flavors are poured into the top of the tank

560
00:36:48,441 --> 00:36:51,640
to be blended with a mix of alcohol, sugar, and color.

561
00:36:52,940 --> 00:36:55,840
The agitator works like a beater used to make a cake.

562
00:36:57,600 --> 00:37:00,780
After agitation, the liqueur will be run through a filter

563
00:37:00,781 --> 00:37:02,320
and then tested.

564
00:37:03,060 --> 00:37:06,060
Once approved by the Hiram Walker Lab,

565
00:37:06,061 --> 00:37:09,060
the liqueurs will then be bottled.

566
00:37:09,061 --> 00:37:13,060
The company ships over 4.7 million cases a year.

567
00:37:13,061 --> 00:37:18,060
Clearly, some liqueurs are more popular than others.

568
00:37:18,061 --> 00:37:23,060
One perennial favorite is the coffee-based Kahlua.

569
00:37:23,061 --> 00:37:26,060
The drink originated in Mexico in the 1930s

570
00:37:26,061 --> 00:37:30,060
and was introduced to the United States in 1962,

571
00:37:30,061 --> 00:37:32,860
1962, where it was an instant success.

572
00:37:33,240 --> 00:37:38,520
It started as a California phenomenon and then moved across the U.S.

573
00:37:38,540 --> 00:37:44,580
And ultimately, after success in the U.S., we took it internationally and it enjoys the international success it has today.

574
00:37:45,420 --> 00:37:49,900
The key to the drink's popularity is the use of gourmet Arabica coffee beans.

575
00:37:50,900 --> 00:37:54,300
We actually source the coffee beans from Veracruz, Mexico.

576
00:37:54,301 --> 00:37:59,700
It's a shade-grown coffee, and it actually produces very small coffee cherries, which

577
00:37:59,701 --> 00:38:04,180
are producing smaller beans, but this allows us to have a more consistent product.

578
00:38:04,181 --> 00:38:07,920
Next, the beans are prepared for processing.

579
00:38:07,921 --> 00:38:10,920
This involves lightly roasting and grinding them.

580
00:38:10,921 --> 00:38:16,020
The coffee is then extracted and blended with sugarcane, sugarcane spirits, vanilla, and

581
00:38:16,021 --> 00:38:22,840
caramel to create the classic drink.

582
00:38:22,841 --> 00:38:29,120
From a medicinal drink made by monks to revolutionizing the cocktail hour, liqueurs have constantly

583
00:38:29,121 --> 00:38:32,440
changed with the times.

584
00:38:32,441 --> 00:38:36,740
In the distilling business, change is ever present.

585
00:38:36,741 --> 00:38:39,800
Everyone is always looking for the next big thing.

586
00:38:39,801 --> 00:38:44,600
Today, some restaurateurs are banking that putting a distillery in their eateries will

587
00:38:44,601 --> 00:38:47,280
be the wave of the future.

588
00:38:47,281 --> 00:38:51,640
Benedictine is believed to be the oldest liqueur in production today.

589
00:38:51,641 --> 00:38:56,400
in 1510 by French monks, its recipe is still a trade secret.

590
00:38:57,460 --> 00:39:01,180
We now return to Distilleries on Modern Marvels.

591
00:39:04,120 --> 00:39:08,980
The alcohol distillation business is one of the most closely regulated in the country.

592
00:39:10,360 --> 00:39:16,140
Most distilleries are big-time operations, employing many and generating millions, both

593
00:39:16,141 --> 00:39:18,360
for their owners and in taxes for the government.

594
00:39:18,361 --> 00:39:25,060
But in some states, restaurants are now allowed to run small distilleries on the premises.

595
00:39:26,200 --> 00:39:32,740
You can try homemade liquor at places like the Dogfish Head Brewing and Eats restaurant in Rehoboth, Delaware.

596
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:38,580
And the Bardinet restaurants in Boise and Eagle, Idaho.

597
00:39:41,500 --> 00:39:45,540
Restaurateurs are banking that distillery restaurants will meet with the same success

598
00:39:45,541 --> 00:39:48,700
that craft brew beer houses had in the late 1980s.

599
00:39:53,320 --> 00:39:55,440
Bardonnay restaurant owner Kevin Settles

600
00:39:55,441 --> 00:39:57,120
got into the field in 2000.

601
00:39:58,020 --> 00:39:59,720
Breweries have been doing it for some time.

602
00:40:00,100 --> 00:40:01,640
I'm not sure, you know, wineries don't do it,

603
00:40:01,700 --> 00:40:03,480
but brew pubs have been very successful,

604
00:40:03,720 --> 00:40:06,080
and so that's really where the idea came from.

605
00:40:07,340 --> 00:40:09,420
In order to make his dream a reality,

606
00:40:09,980 --> 00:40:11,420
Settles knew he would have to go through

607
00:40:11,421 --> 00:40:13,060
a lot of governmental red tape.

608
00:40:13,760 --> 00:40:15,280
I spent about two or three years

609
00:40:15,281 --> 00:40:17,580
reading through the federal regulations,

610
00:40:17,581 --> 00:40:20,780
trying to figure out what regulations would stop me

611
00:40:20,781 --> 00:40:22,520
from being able to do this.

612
00:40:22,521 --> 00:40:24,280
Through all that research, I learned that really

613
00:40:24,281 --> 00:40:27,160
what the government cares about is the taxes.

614
00:40:27,161 --> 00:40:29,700
Taxes on distilled spirits are very high.

615
00:40:29,701 --> 00:40:33,300
I knew how to make sure that the process that we set up here

616
00:40:33,301 --> 00:40:35,700
would allow them to be comfortable that they were

617
00:40:35,701 --> 00:40:38,240
getting every dime due them, and that every product that we

618
00:40:38,241 --> 00:40:42,600
made would adhere to the federal regulations.

619
00:40:42,601 --> 00:40:44,540
After getting the state and federal government's

620
00:40:44,541 --> 00:40:48,631
approval. The next test was getting the approval of his customers.

621
00:40:48,632 --> 00:40:50,180
With distilling, it's not

622
00:40:50,181 --> 00:40:55,120
something you can practice at home, and it really takes a long time to learn how to make a good

623
00:40:55,121 --> 00:41:00,020
product, and having it right here in the bar, we could set that drink on the counter, and if the

624
00:41:00,021 --> 00:41:04,860
customer made a nasty face, we could pull it right back and set the product aside and get back to

625
00:41:04,861 --> 00:41:11,039
work on it. One of the most popular liquors made in-house is Bardinet rum.

626
00:41:11,040 --> 00:41:12,960
Distiller Scott Probert

627
00:41:12,961 --> 00:41:14,060
uses a pot still.

628
00:41:15,180 --> 00:41:16,720
This is an eau de vise still,

629
00:41:16,800 --> 00:41:20,420
so it's made for processing brandies and eau de vise,

630
00:41:20,660 --> 00:41:23,240
and it's also capable of producing whiskeys

631
00:41:23,241 --> 00:41:26,300
and rums, very heavily flavored spirits.

632
00:41:27,820 --> 00:41:30,300
All Bardinet spirits are made from scratch.

633
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:33,260
Now I'm going to take my brown cane sugar.

634
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:37,020
Now this is a luxury item in distillation,

635
00:41:37,480 --> 00:41:39,320
especially for making rum.

636
00:41:39,600 --> 00:41:40,740
It ferments clean,

637
00:41:40,741 --> 00:41:43,500
creates a nice base for neutral spirits.

638
00:41:45,160 --> 00:41:46,460
So I'm going to take this sugar,

639
00:41:47,840 --> 00:41:49,000
pour it into the bucket.

640
00:41:52,900 --> 00:41:54,600
Now I'm going to stir it up

641
00:41:54,601 --> 00:41:56,160
to make sure all the sugars are dissolved

642
00:41:56,161 --> 00:41:57,220
for proper fermentation.

643
00:42:02,400 --> 00:42:04,120
So once this is dissolved properly,

644
00:42:04,520 --> 00:42:05,860
now I'm going to pour it into the tank

645
00:42:05,861 --> 00:42:08,720
where it'll ferment for 10 to 14 days.

646
00:42:10,740 --> 00:42:13,580
After fermentation, the mixture is sent to the still.

647
00:42:14,500 --> 00:42:17,840
The whole process for rum is approximately two to three weeks.

648
00:42:18,560 --> 00:42:21,900
The distiller has to remain vigilant during the entire process.

649
00:42:22,460 --> 00:42:27,200
So the biggest thing, then, in producing a consistent product is the taste.

650
00:42:27,800 --> 00:42:32,640
You need to be standing by the still, taking note of any minor changes that are occurring.

651
00:42:33,060 --> 00:42:38,020
You're less likely to have any inconsistencies in your run if you keep everything the same.

652
00:42:38,021 --> 00:42:44,160
Just like at big distilleries, it's up to the distiller's nose and knowledge of his product

653
00:42:44,161 --> 00:42:47,160
to judge that the liquor is up to Bardinet standards.

654
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:54,220
Once Probert runs a batch, he blends it and adds color.

655
00:42:58,400 --> 00:43:00,620
Next, the rum is bottled by hand.

656
00:43:02,220 --> 00:43:04,620
The liquor doesn't go directly to the bar.

657
00:43:04,621 --> 00:43:08,200
Instead, it must be sent to a state liquor store,

658
00:43:08,340 --> 00:43:10,920
where it's stamped and processed for taxation.

659
00:43:13,280 --> 00:43:16,400
Then the Bardonnay restaurant must buy it back from the state

660
00:43:16,401 --> 00:43:18,360
in order to sell it to their customers.

661
00:43:21,720 --> 00:43:24,880
The distilled in-house, homegrown Idaho liquor

662
00:43:24,881 --> 00:43:27,400
has proven to be a hit at the restaurant.

663
00:43:28,720 --> 00:43:31,180
I've tried numerous drinks,

664
00:43:31,181 --> 00:43:34,780
and I think that they've just done a wonderful job.

665
00:43:35,320 --> 00:43:36,280
Well, it's very unique.

666
00:43:36,420 --> 00:43:37,340
We saw them build it.

667
00:43:37,440 --> 00:43:39,740
We saw the distillery go in,

668
00:43:39,880 --> 00:43:43,100
and not only is it fun to look at what they've done back there,

669
00:43:43,200 --> 00:43:45,040
but the liquor is very, very good.

670
00:43:45,300 --> 00:43:48,480
They just have a uniqueness that you don't find in most restaurants.

671
00:43:48,900 --> 00:43:50,220
It took about two months, three months,

672
00:43:50,240 --> 00:43:51,360
for people to know we were here,

673
00:43:51,480 --> 00:43:53,740
and then it's been successful ever since.

674
00:43:54,900 --> 00:43:55,760
So cheers.

675
00:43:56,780 --> 00:43:59,820
Whether it's an oldie but goodie like cognac...

676
00:43:59,821 --> 00:44:03,820
or a trendy new liqueur.

677
00:44:03,821 --> 00:44:08,820
They're all the end results of people trying to put magic in a bottle.

